GAFF: van der Waals gradient without 0/0 for zero-radius pairs

The VdW gradient was written 12 Eab / RVDWab * (term7 - term13). For a
pair whose radii sum to zero -- two hydroxyl hydrogens, GAFF type ho,
R = 0 and eps = 0 -- that is 0/0 = NaN, while the pair's energy is a plain
0. Any molecule with two OH groups got a NaN gradient on those hydrogens;
L-BFGS (whose line search does not zero NaN directions) then found no
lower energy and stopped after one step, reporting convergence (taxol:
1 step at E 662 where CG reaches ~470). Use the equal
12 Eab (term6 - term12) / r.

diff --git a/src/forcefields/forcefieldgaff.cpp b/src/forcefields/forcefieldgaff.cpp
index 242619d73..d2375ad80 100644
--- a/src/forcefields/forcefieldgaff.cpp
+++ b/src/forcefields/forcefieldgaff.cpp
@@ -369,9 +369,12 @@ namespace OpenBabel
     energy = Eab * (term12 - 2.0*term6);
 
     if (gradients) {
-      const double term13 = term * term12; // ^13
-      const double term7 = term * term6; // ^7
-      const double dE = (12.0 * Eab / RVDWab) * (-term13 + term7);
+      // dE/dr = 12 Eab (term6 - term12) / r. Written as 12 Eab / RVDWab times
+      // (term7 - term13), as it was, it is 0/0 = NaN for a pair whose radii
+      // sum to zero -- two hydroxyl hydrogens (type ho, R = 0, eps = 0) --
+      // although the pair's energy is a plain 0. One NaN poisons the whole
+      // gradient: L-BFGS then finds no step and stops where it started.
+      const double dE = 12.0 * Eab * (term6 - term12) / rab;
       OBForceField::VectorSelfMultiply(force_a, dE);
       OBForceField::VectorSelfMultiply(force_b, dE);
     }
